    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "i checked the bucket policy and there aren't any Deny statements in there. everything looks super clean. i'm also not using any VPC endpoints or anything complicated, it's just a standard setup. \n\nit's weird because it works fine when i try to upload a file manually through the console, but the lambda just hits a wall every time. it feels like something is specifically guarding the entrance or something lol. do you think it could be an encryption issue? like maybe s3 kms settings?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Encryption is a very strong possibility, especially if the console upload works but the programmatic call fails. If your S3 bucket is configured to enforce server-side encryption (SSE-S3 or SSE-KMS), the Lambda function needs to explicitly request or provide the correct encryption headers when it calls `PutObject`. If it doesn't, S3 will reject the write request with an \"Access Denied\" error, even if the IAM policy is perfect.\n\nIf you are using KMS, the Lambda's execution role must *also* have the necessary `kms:Decrypt` and `kms:GenerateDataKey` permissions on the specific KMS key used by the bucket. Try adding the appropriate `x-amz-server-side-encryption` header to your Lambda's S3 request to see if that resolves the issue.",
      "source": "target",
